# How to Use This Volume

## What this volume is

This volume contains **three complete prediction papers** for the CSS Gender Studies examination, each set exactly as the **Federal Public Service Commission** prints the paper: the Roll Number box, the heading, the time-and-marks lines, the printed **NOTE**, **Part-I** (Q. No. 1, twenty MCQs with options (a) to (d)) and **Part-II** (Q. No. 2 to Q. No. 8, attempt any four, 20 marks each, with one question of short notes).

::: table | The three sets at a glance.
@widths 2.2,4.4,7.4
Set | Design | What it tests
^^Set 1^^ | The most probable paper | The questions the record makes most likely for the next paper: the families that rotate back, the blind spots most likely to break, and the 2023–2026 style of long, current stems
^^Set 2^^ | The difficult paper: about 70% difficult, 30% medium | Theory-heavy and cross-topic questions — historicizing constructionism, men's feminism, the political economy of adjustment, Galtung, the "Western import" debate — that separate high scorers from the rest
^^Set 3^^ | The mixed paper | Probable and medium questions alongside difficult ones — the shape of most real papers — covering the lines the first two sets leave out
:::

## How each set is built

1. **The paper** — Part-I and Part-II, set as FPSC prints them. Nothing in the paper gives the answer away.
2. **Part I explained** — every MCQ again, with its four options and the **correct answer in bold straight after it**, followed by a one-line reason.
3. **The model answers** — one for every question in Part II, including **every** short note. Each model answer follows the house format of the Question Answers: the question in a box, an **answer map**, **decoding the question**, the **model answer** with numbered headings, sub-headings and sub-sub-headings, at least one **figure** besides the map, and **why this answer scores**.

## How to sit a paper

::: eye | Sit each paper as if it were the real examination
1. **Part I — 30 minutes.** Answer all twenty MCQs on a separate sheet. Do not look at "Part I explained" until you have finished.
2. **Choose — 10 minutes.** Read all seven questions of Part II twice and choose the four where you have both **theory** and **Pakistani evidence**.
3. **Write — about 30 minutes per question.** Draw a quick answer map in the margin, then write 1,000–1,300 words under numbered headings, with at least one diagram.
4. **Mark yourself.** Compare each answer with the model: did you answer every limb of the question? Did each heading carry a thinker, a law or a dated figure? Did you include a figure, a Pakistani angle and a judgement?
5. **Space the papers out.** Sit Set 1 three weeks before the examination, Set 3 two weeks before and Set 2 one week before.
:::

# How the Papers Were Set

## The method

No one can predict a paper. But the eleven papers from ==2016== to ==2026== show clear patterns, analysed in the Master Anatomy, and the three sets are built on them:

1. **The rotation of families.** The examiner returns to a small number of question families — gender-based violence, WID/WAD/GAD, the waves, the movement in Pakistan, development theories, social construction, quotas — in new forms.
2. **The blind spots.** Syllabus lines never asked as a descriptive question are filled sooner or later. Every Tier 3 blind spot of the Master Anatomy appears at least once across the three sets.
3. **The contemporary layer.** Since 2024 the examiner has named reports, laws and figures from the previous year or two. Every set includes current material: the 2025–2026 laws, the Global Gender Gap reports of 2025 and 2026, the 2024 elections, Beijing+30, the IMF programmes of 2023–2024 and the digital gender gap.
4. **The objective paper's shift to theory.** The MCQs are weighted like the 2023–2026 papers — more concepts and thinkers than dates — with several keys of "None of these", as in 2026.
5. **Topics 1, 4 and 7 in every set**, as the Master Anatomy's format requires.

## Every descriptive question, and why it was set

::: table | The 25 descriptive items across the three sets.
@widths 1.6,1,6.6,4.8
Set · Q | Topic | Question (in short) | Why it was set
1 · 2 | T1 | Women's to Gender Studies; status in Pakistan; autonomy versus integration | The discipline half of T1, asked four times, silent since 2023
1 · 3 | T7 | The domestic-violence laws, 2013–2025, and why legislation alone fails | GBV in 9 of 11 papers; the Islamabad law of 2025 completes the set
1 · 4 | T4 | Gender mainstreaming: tools, achievements, limits | The next angle on WID/WAD/GAD; never asked in full
1 · 5 | T3 | The fourth wave, #MeToo and the Aurat March | The waves asked five times; the fourth wave is the next step
1 · 6 | T5 | Women's education and Article 25A | The clearest blind spot in the paper
1 · 7 | T6 | Women as voters and candidates; Elections Act 2017; 2024 elections | A blind spot with rich, current data
1 · 8(a) | T2 | Liberal feminism | The most famous school, never asked alone
1 · 8(b) | T3 | The UN World Conferences and Beijing+30 | Never a descriptive question; Beijing+30 in 2025
2 · 2 | T1 | Historicizing constructionism; is the sex/gender distinction constructed? | A syllabus line never asked by name; follows the 2024 and 2026 questions
2 · 3 | T2 | Men's feminism, Connell and the manosphere | Never asked in either part of the paper
2 · 4 | T4 | Structural adjustment, IMF programmes 2023–2024 and the care economy | Asked once (2022); IMF programmes make it topical
2 · 5 | T7 | Honour killing through Galtung's triangle; the 2004 and 2016 laws | Galtung named in 2019; honour killing a constant
2 · 6 | T3 | "Feminism is a Western import" | The most common objection to feminism in Pakistan; joins T2 and T3
2 · 7 | T5 | Family law: MFLO 1961 and the child-marriage laws | Women and law asked directly only once; new laws of 2025–2026
2 · 8(a) | T6 | Gender and good governance | Defining governance has never been asked
2 · 8(b) | T4 | World-system theory and the sexual division of labour | Asked only as a partner of other theories (2019, 2025)
3 · 2 | T1 | Gender roles, stereotypes and the agents that make them | A reliable construction question in a new, applied form
3 · 3 | T4 | BISP through Moser and Kabeer | GAD applied to Pakistan's largest programme
3 · 4 | T2 | Patriarchy: radical, Marxist and socialist explanations | Schools of feminism recur as full questions and notes
3 · 5 | T5 | Women's paid work (24.4%) and unpaid care | Asked in 2019; new LFS data
3 · 6 | T6 | Reserved seats and substantive representation | Quotas asked six times; a harder evaluative angle
3 · 7 | T7 | Mukhtaran Mai, Malala and Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy compared | Each asked singly; the comparison never
3 · 8(a) | T7 | Technology-facilitated violence | The digital layer of the 2026 paper
3 · 8(b) | T4 | SDG 5 and Pakistan's progress | The SDGs never asked in full
3 · 8(c) | T5 | The National Commission on the Status of Women | An MCQ favourite, never a descriptive question
:::

